{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>Behold, a people, etc.</b> Its saying <b>הֶן</b> is along the lines of what our Sages of blessed memory said (Shabbat 119b): a wicked messenger answers amen involuntarily — for Balaam himself said הֵן, thus: so may this people be — כְּלָבִיא יָקוּם, וְכַאֲרִי, etc. And its saying <b>כְּלָבִיא</b> means: his first rising is not like the rest of the world, who at the outset, being lacking in the training of warfare, do not perform mighty deeds; rather, כְּלָבִיא יָקוּם — he rises and performs wonders. And so it was: even though from of old he was a captive in a pit-house, afflicted with human suffering, nevertheless upon his going forth from the city he performed wonders against Sihon and Og. He further said <b>וְכַאֲרִי יִתְנַשָּׂא</b> — meaning, along the lines of what our Sages of blessed memory said (Shabbat 77b): three [things], as long as they age, increase in strength — the lion, etc. And this is its saying <b>יִתְנַשָּׂא</b>: he lifts himself up and exalts in his strength, such that even if several wars pass over him, they will not thereby be weakened but will go on and grow stronger." "<b>Further,</b> it intends that besides the strength that HaShem placed in them, they will still be, in the eyes of all the nations, on a great level [of being] feared by them, as it says (Joshua 2:24): ״וְגַם נָמוֹגוּ״ etc. And this is its saying <b>כְּלָבִיא יָקוּם</b> — this is in the dimension of his might, and in the dimension of what will be accounted in the eyes of the world <b>וְכַאֲרִי יִתְנַשָּׂא</b> — meaning, more than what they are already exalted in the eyes of the world." "<b>He will not lie down, etc.</b> — meaning that the night offers no escape to flee from him, for night before him shines like day. And go forth and learn from Joshua (Joshua 10:12–13), who told the sun to stand still and did not hasten to set, and the like. And this is its saying <b>לֹא יִשְׁכַּב עַד יֹאכַל טֶרֶף</b> — from this you learn that at the time of lying down he is occupied with eating prey." "And its saying <b>וְדַם חֲלָלִים יִשְׁתֶּה</b> — for by human nature, when one kills many casualties, the soul of those who remain becomes precious in his eyes. Therefore he said: woe to the nations, for this people regard blood like a beverage that one drinks; and blood is not reckoned in their eyes as an act of killing but as an act of drinking." "And he was precise to say <b>יִשְׁתֶּה</b>, in accordance with what we have already introduced: that through the killing of the nations, the parts of holiness adhere to the people of HaShem, in the sod of ״כִּי לַחְמֵנוּ הֵם״ (Numbers 14:9). So too he said here ״יִשְׁתֶּה״ — and understand. And our Sages of blessed memory (Ḥullin 35b) learned from here regarding blood that is considered a beverage."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:13:36.494675152Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}